  "slug": "causal-neural-function-vivo-4b72",
  "summary": "Preprints and one published study link neonatal, genetic and maternal exposures to cell-specific neurovascular and neural changes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is cell-resolved readout of neural and neurovascular compartments following a defined perturbation or genetic risk variant. Neonatal SARS-CoV-2 spike protein exposure in mice produced region-specific changes in SOX2-positive cells and TBR1 neurons at P10, without broad cerebrovascular or BBB alterations. In iPSC-derived models, the NOTCH3 p.R544C CADASIL variant was associated with early BBB failure, complement-matrix activation and metabolic dysregulation, while blood-brain barrier cells from 22q11.2 deletion carriers showed structural and metabolic differences linked to schizophrenia penetrance. The published study reports that maternal TPOAb positivity in mice was associated with placental inflammation, fetal BBB disruption, hippocampal microglial activation and altered BDNF/synaptic signaling. What is new is the focus on specific barrier and neural cell populations, including progenitors, neurons and microglia, across rodent and human-derived systems. Most evidence remains preprint-stage and associative.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
